# Chapter00 canonical source code review

Final source: 4895c1a6a94660a43e617ed86a21844838a7324d, base 652d3b2476416a1ead7332f12055d1a18018cfb6. Read-only review; all copies, builds and input mutations are isolated under this evidence directory. `snapshot.json` records the original 15 filesystem rows (rename represented by deletion/addition); `final-head.json` independently binds every row to the final commit. No new blocking code finding.

The moved 14026-byte Doc source is exactly the reviewed cd451bf manuscript and the base authored file, SHA256 48c2b1696c7308ee43f26dbf06a6038b38f0199233c1da3ef15a04453f810e93. The 4368-byte historical Markdown is exactly both a163764e93e2809f8013d7bbe16721082f979143 and base spec00, SHA256 4ca8d780d32696bf102774c346ffdf18d01452085f93a2daae9e49acf16b003f. Existing generated Doc fixture remains byte-identical. The converter generate/sentence/quoted ASTs are unchanged; the Rust historical comparison changes only test name and include path, preserving explicit expected output and event comparison. Thus this is not regeneration of the old expected value to accommodate new annotated Markdown.

The converter CLI checks the pinned LF-normalized historical source hash before either generated output or audit write. Four independent changed-input cases (code spelling, added section, current annotated Markdown, UTF-8 BOM) reject even with --write and preserve both existing sentinels. CRLF checkout normalization is intentionally retained and regenerates exactly the original LF fixture. This is a pin on the historical CLI input, not a claim that the general generate helper accepts only one source. Audit keys now identify a historical fixture; active consumers do not require removed canonical audit keys. The frozen inventory remains an initial audit, not current canonical ownership, per spec16.

Registry00 binds the moved source, original Markdown URL, separate alias input, docs/spec/00-contract.html and existing renderer /2. Registry13 is unchanged. Seven aliases match the seven actual heading anchors in the preserved old GitHub HTML, and each emitted name anchor occurs exactly once. Six semantic IDs are still emitted. The title's reviewed wording change is covered by an explicit legacy alias. Navigation of the new public GitHub or local HTML is outside this code record and is being reviewed separately.

Prior review records were read directly, not replaced by author preflight conclusions. The initial independent review checked mandatory meaning and found five missing-Ruby segments in chapter00; the independent cd451bf correction review verified those fixes without base-text changes. Current source equals that reviewed corrected blob. All 18 author-preflight payload hashes were checked. This preserves the prior review scope and does not infer new human screen-reader operation, global migration completion or J01-J04 completion.

Independent verification on a fixed Git-blob checkout: Python unittest 4 passed; default historical converter check passed; production Rust `projection::historical_contract_projects_to_equivalent_markdown_events --exact` ran exactly 1 test and passed; production `doc-canonical --check` passed for both contract and reproducibility. Three additional actual chapter00 mutations (hand-edited generated Markdown, removed legacy alias, changed Doc title text) failed as stale generated Markdown and did not write any input. `canonical-boundaries.json` records the actual binary SHA and commands. No HTML/browser success is inferred from these checks.

The first isolated Rust build omitted examples used by compile-time include_str and failed before test execution. Its original logs remain; `dependency-addendum.json` records the exact-base example inputs supplied before the successful retry. This differs from the root's separately reported initial zero-test filter invocation, which is not counted here. A scratch alias assertion initially expected id rather than the actual reviewed name attribute; inspection corrected that review assertion, with no production change. Only Windows native host execution was performed for this bounded code delta; no Unix or WASI rerun is claimed. Core/parser/renderer runtime implementation is unchanged from the reviewed base.

`workspace` and `target` are replay conveniences, excluded from this manifest. Sources, exact dependency hashes, old inputs, scripts, raw command logs and results are retained. Reconstruct from base Git blobs plus source overlay and dependency addendum; scripts that mutate inputs do so only inside the isolated workspace and restore them in finally blocks. Re-running check.py against a later live dirty tree would replace the initial snapshot, so use the recorded final commit rather than treating the snapshot script as a read-only verifier.
